  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>Quantity of Electricity&nbsp;<math> <mrow> <mo>=</mo> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn>1</mn> <mn>0</mn> <mo>&#215;</mo> <mn>3</mn> <mn>6</mn> <mn>0</mn> <mn>0</mn> <mo>&#215;</mo> <mn>2</mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mn>9</mn> <mn>6</mn> <mn>5</mn> <mn>0</mn> <mn>0</mn> </mrow> </mfrac> </mrow> </math><br />&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.746</mo><mo>&#8776;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.75</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></math>.<br />If oxidation state is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></math> for&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mol</mi></math> deposition of a metal:<br />Electricity<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mi>nF</mi></math> (Faradays)<br />For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>250</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mol</mi></math><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>0.250</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>nF</mo></math><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8756;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>0.250</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>nF</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0.75</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>F</mo></math><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></math></p></blockquote>"
